 "segments": [
  {
   "s": 0.0,
   "e": 4.04,
   "t": "an IQ of 145, the mind that changed the FBI forever."
  },
  {
   "s": 4.74,
   "e": 12.44,
   "t": "In 1978, FBI Special Agents Robert Restler and John Douglas entered the prison cell at Vacaville."
  },
  {
   "s": 13.48,
   "e": 18.18,
   "t": "Kemper filled the doorway like a mountain, but he smiled politely and spoke with the measured"
  },
  {
   "s": 18.18,
   "e": 25.08,
   "t": "cadence of a professor. Possessing an IQ of 145, he demanded no plea deals or lawyers,"
  },
  {
   "s": 25.08,
   "e": 28.14,
   "t": "dissecting his inner darkness with clinical precision."
  },
  {
   "s": 29.1,
   "e": 32.06,
   "t": "Kemper explained a revolutionary concept to the FBI,"
  },
  {
   "s": 32.62,
   "e": 34.1,
   "t": "the violent fantasy cycle."
  },
  {
   "s": 35.14,
   "e": 37.5,
   "t": "Homicide does not begin when weapons are drawn,"
  },
  {
   "s": 37.86,
   "e": 40.88,
   "t": "but in hundreds of mental rehearsals conducted over years."
  },
  {
   "s": 41.9,
   "e": 44.78,
   "t": "He revealed why he took trophies, not to boast,"
  },
  {
   "s": 44.98,
   "e": 47.9,
   "t": "but as catalysts to re-experience supreme power"
  },
  {
   "s": 47.9,
   "e": 50.1,
   "t": "once post-crime euphoria faded."
  },
  {
   "s": 51.2,
   "e": 53.78,
   "t": "Dialogues with Kemper and 35 other felons"
  },
  {
   "s": 53.78,
   "e": 58.56,
   "t": "formed the foundation for FBI-organized offender classification and the VyCAP network."
  },
  {
   "s": 59.46,
   "e": 64.58,
   "t": "Time scenes leave more than physical clues, they leave the unique psychological signature of the"
  },
  {
   "s": 64.58,
   "e": 69.94,
   "t": "perpetrator. This dossier became the defining cornerstone for criminal behavioral analysis"
  },
  {
   "s": 69.94,
   "e": 76.82,
   "t": "and the inspiration behind Mindhunter. Episode 3 of Amber Eyes, watch the full dossier via the"
  },
  {
   "s": 76.82,
   "e": 77.44,
   "t": "link in our bio."
  }
 ]
}